WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
A receptor has let go, yet a G protein remains active.

2
00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:07.580
What decides when that already-loaded switch stops?

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.900
Follow that molecule beneath the membrane and watch its active interval.

4
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:17.400
The answer lies inside the nucleotide the protein carries.

5
00:00:20.983 --> 00:00:22.323
Start with the switch off.

6
00:00:23.603 --> 00:00:27.723
It holds GDP, shown as a slate token with its phosphate tabs.

7
00:00:28.963 --> 00:00:33.183
The protein grips that token tightly, so exchange is slow without help.

8
00:00:34.363 --> 00:00:37.903
That resting complex waits beside the receptor in the membrane.

9
00:00:39.033 --> 00:00:44.673
An occupied receptor acts as a guanine nucleotide exchange factor, or GEF.

10
00:00:45.793 --> 00:00:48.593
It opens the grip and promotes GDP release.

11
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:54.593
GTP, far more plentiful than GDP, then takes the empty place.

12
00:00:55.733 --> 00:00:59.433
The receptor promotes exchange; it does not hand over GTP.

13
00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:03.213
GTP changes the protein's shape.

14
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:09.673
The block turns gold, its activity trace rises, and a target beside it switches on.

15
00:01:10.953 --> 00:01:14.073
This GTP-bound interval is the state we will time.

16
00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:19.673
Alpha and beta-gamma can each regulate targets after the rearrangement.

17
00:01:20.683 --> 00:01:24.243
The G protein is also an enzyme, a GTPase.

18
00:01:25.403 --> 00:01:29.083
After a variable wait, it cuts off GTP's last phosphate.

19
00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:34.083
GDP remains, the shape relaxes, and the target turns off.

20
00:01:35.603 --> 00:01:38.683
Cutting always returns this switch to its GDP-bound state.

21
00:01:39.733 --> 00:01:42.253
Here is the rule for that loaded molecule.

22
00:01:43.953 --> 00:01:48.393
Faster GTP cutting shortens an already-loaded GTPase's time on.

23
00:01:49.733 --> 00:01:52.593
Loading starts the interval; hydrolysis ends it.

24
00:01:53.673 --> 00:01:56.153
The rule does not say how often loading begins.

25
00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.223
Now compare matched gold switches.

26
00:02:00.483 --> 00:02:04.803
A GAP approaches only the lower switch and accelerates its cutting reaction.

27
00:02:06.083 --> 00:02:08.543
Both begin with the same GTP and target.

28
00:02:09.923 --> 00:02:11.583
Which gold interval should end sooner?

29
00:02:12.883 --> 00:02:13.883
Hold your answer.

30
00:02:19.667 --> 00:02:21.427
The lower interval ends first.

31
00:02:22.747 --> 00:02:25.667
The GAP does not remove GTP or supply fuel.

32
00:02:26.947 --> 00:02:32.367
It helps the GTPase reach the cutting transition faster, then leaves GDP behind.

33
00:02:33.627 --> 00:02:36.487
The products are unchanged; only the speed differs.

34
00:02:37.617 --> 00:02:42.777
For first-order cutting, the average active time is the reciprocal of the cutting

35
00:02:42.777 --> 00:02:47.937
rate constant. A larger rate constant means a shorter mean interval.

36
00:02:49.197 --> 00:02:51.757
The exact wait still varies among molecules.

37
00:02:53.257 --> 00:02:55.997
Fewer molecules remain active at each later time.

38
00:02:57.017 --> 00:02:59.237
A whole-cell response has other clocks.

39
00:03:00.717 --> 00:03:05.537
Receptors can reload many switches, targets can turn off, and messengers can be

40
00:03:05.537 --> 00:03:09.677
cleared. Those clocks also shape the pathway-level trace.

41
00:03:11.077 --> 00:03:14.817
Our rule isolates a single already-loaded GTPase's lifetime.

42
00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.740
That distinction makes experiments readable.

43
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.060
Change loading, and you change how often switches start.

44
00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:29.200
Change GTP cutting, and you change how long each loaded switch remains active.

45
00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:34.460
Keeping those controls separate prevents a common reasoning error.
